ozz-archive-counts: refuse an archive whose versions or counts ozz cannot hold

Upstream ozz reads each count from the archive and sizes an allocation from
it without a check: Skeleton::Load takes an int32 joint count and character
count (a negative one wraps the buffer size), Animation::Load eleven uint32
counts, Track::Load an int32 name length, and every vector and string a
uint32 size. An unsupported version is logged and the object left empty,
which reads the same as a valid empty one; RawTrack only asserts it.

IArchive gains a refusal record (Refuse, refused, refusal_what, _value,
_limit and _past_end), the bytes left in its stream (remaining), a count
check against a limit and those bytes (CheckCount) and a nesting bound for
recursive types (Enter/Leave). Every Load checks its version and each count
against ozz's own limits (Skeleton::kMaxJoints, the uint16 timepoint range
Animation::Allocate asserts, at least one name byte per skeleton joint) and
the bytes left before it allocates, and refuses through the record; every
vector and string Load checks its size before it resizes. refusal_past_end
tells a count past the bytes left (the archive ends before what it claims)
from one past a limit. Once refused, the archive reads nothing more: a
primitive loads as zero, binary data as zeros, an object not at all, and
every check fails, so enclosing Loads unwind without reading past the
refusal. RawSkeleton's joints nest no deeper than kMaxJoints.

Evidence: zozz's archive fuzz property, mutated fixture skeletons, faults in
Skeleton::Load (skeleton.cc:149, 152) with "applying non-zero offset to null
pointer" under UBSan and an access violation in release (measured,
2026-10-31). The minimal inputs are src/testing/archives.zig's.

